 <p>Note: This is a complex area behind the scenes, to handle the changes that may result to the difference on exchange. The system calculates the alteration to the exchange difference which results from allocating the receipt to different invoices (or not being allocated at all) and creates the necessary journal in the general ledger - if the integration option is used - to ensure the debtors control account stays in balance with the list of balances.</p>
 
 <h4>It is recommended that help be sought if alterations in the program are required in this area.</h4><!-- Help End: CustomerAllocations -->
+<!-- Help Begin: CustomerAdjustment -->
+
+<div class="floatright">
+    <a class="minitext" href="#top">⬆ Top</a>
+</div>
+
+<h2><a id="CustomerAdjustment">Customer Balance Adjustments And Write-Offs</a></h2>
+
+<p>A customer's balance can be corrected directly, without raising a fake credit note or asking for a journal. The adjustment is a customer transaction (of type "Journal - Debtors") in the customer's currency. It appears on the customer's account, inquiry and statement, in the aged debtors and balance reports, and in the General Ledger. It can be allocated to invoices and receipts like any other customer transaction. Only users whose security role has the security token "Customer balance adjustments and write-offs" (token 21) can use these pages; by default this is the System Administrator role and further roles can be given the token in Security Roles. Without the token the menu links and the link on the customer screen are not available.</p>
+
+<h3>Adjusting a balance</h3>
+
+<p>From the accounts receivable tab choose "Adjust or Write Off a Customer Balance", or select the customer and use "Adjust or Write Off This Customer Balance" in the Customer Transactions box on the customer screen. Choose whether the adjustment increases or decreases the amount owed by the customer and enter:</p>
+
+<ul>
+    <li>the amount, always a positive number in the customer's currency; the choice of increase or decrease sets the direction,</li>
+    <li>the date of the adjustment, which also determines the accounting period; as for other transactions, a date in a period that is closed by the "Prohibit GL Journals Before" setting is posted in the first open period,</li>
+    <li>the reason, which is mandatory and is kept with the transaction and in the General Ledger narrative,</li>
+    <li>the General Ledger account the other side of the adjustment is posted to (for example bad debts expense) when the debtors ledger is integrated with the General Ledger. The debtors control account and bank accounts cannot be used.</li>
+</ul>
+
+<p>An increase debits the debtors control account and credits the chosen account, a decrease does the opposite. The amount is converted to the home currency at the current exchange rate of the customer's currency. A decrease is a credit on the account that is not matched to anything; use "Allocate Credits To A Customer's Account" (a link is offered after posting) to match it to invoices. An increase is a debit that can be matched with receipts in the same way.</p>
+
+<h3>Writing off what is left on an invoice</h3>
+
+<p>The same page lists the customer's open invoices. Click "Write off balance" against an invoice, give the date, the reason and the General Ledger account and confirm. A decrease adjustment for the whole amount outstanding on the invoice is posted at the exchange rate of the invoice (so there is no exchange difference) and is allocated to the invoice, which is then settled.</p>
+
+<h3>Clean-up of small balances</h3>
+
+<p>"Write Off Small Customer Balances" (accounts receivable tab) writes off in one go every customer invoice that is left open with a balance less than an amount you choose. The amount is in the home currency; the balance of invoices in other currencies is converted at the rate of each invoice. Enter the amount, the date, the reason and the General Ledger account and choose "List the Invoices". The invoices that would be written off are shown and nothing is posted until you confirm. Each invoice gets its own adjustment, as when writing off a single invoice, and either all of them are written off or, if there is an error, none.</p>
+
+<p>Credit notes, receipts and other credits that are left unallocated are not touched by the clean-up.</p><!-- Help End: CustomerAdjustment -->
